1
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,080
Now let's get started

2
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:10,560
And today we will be finishing up model-based reinforcement

3
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,280
learning, specifically a way to use a learned model

4
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:16,239
with synthetic data generation and when

5
00:00:16,239 --> 00:00:18,480
to use model-based reinforcement learning

6
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,640
And then we're going to be talking about multitask

7
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,360
imitation learning and reinforcement

8
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:27,760
learning, including ways to conditions on tasks

9
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,320
different tasks as goal-reaching tasks

10
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:33,100
and an approach called hindsight relabeling

11
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,600
which you'll be implementing

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,560
Cool

13
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:40,020
So let's get back to model-based reinforcement learning

14
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:42,960
So last time, we talked about a scenario

15
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,160
where we learned some model

16
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,300
That model could be a deterministic model

17
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:55,000
It could also be modeling a distribution over next states

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,040
And in particular, you could think about it

19
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,950
as trying to approximate the distribution

20
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:07,590
over the next state, given the current state and action

21
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:12,230
And you can use this model to actually predict

22
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:13,730
what will happen in the future

23
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:17,510
And for example, if you have an action that you think

24
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:19,150
you might take or multiple actions

25
00:01:19,150 --> 00:01:20,630
you think you might take, you can

26
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:26,910
use this estimated distribution to generate or imagine

27
00:01:26,910 --> 00:01:28,790
what might happen in the future if you

28
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:32,510
were to take those actions

29
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:36,110
We talked about an approach to behavior

30
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:38,390
that's quite different from learning a policy

31
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:40,990
where we're not actually explicitly learning

32
00:01:40,990 --> 00:01:44,950
a mapping from states to actions and instead, on the fly

33
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:47,950
we are thinking through, for a given state

34
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:49,470
what actions we should take

35
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,150
And we called this planning

36
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:53,470
And we talked about how this sort of planning

37
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:57,630
can be done with gradient-based or sampling-based optimization

38
00:01:57,630 --> 00:02:00,810
over the next future horizon

39
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:02,230
Then we also talked about how it's

40
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:05,670
important to update the model using the data collected

41
00:02:05,670 --> 00:02:08,030
because then you'll be able to actually learn

42
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:12,930
a better model in the realms that you want to use that model

43
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:14,470
And then periodically, we also talked

44
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:17,850
about how you can replan to help account for mistakes

45
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,270
account for errors in the model, and also react

46
00:02:22,270 --> 00:02:25,790
to changes in the environment

47
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:26,360
Cool

48
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:28,110
The pros of this sort of planning approach

49
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:29,130
is that it's simple

50
00:02:29,130 --> 00:02:31,910
It's easy to plug in different goals or rewards

51
00:02:31,910 --> 00:02:34,550
if you have a general enough model, possibly

52
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:36,030
even at test time

53
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:39,130
And the downside is that it's compute intensive at test time

54
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:42,470
It requires a lot of test time compute at inference time

55
00:02:42,470 --> 00:02:45,110
And this particular instantiation

56
00:02:45,110 --> 00:02:49,190
was only practical for short-horizon problems

57
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:52,470
or for very shaped rewards, where you can plan over

58
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:55,870
some shorter horizon rather than considering

59
00:02:55,870 --> 00:03:01,500
an extremely long horizon

60
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,940
And there's two reasons why this is only

61
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:06,200
applicable to short horizons

62
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,580
One is that as you increase the horizon

63
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,040
it becomes even more compute intensive

64
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,860
And the second reason is that the model is not very accurate

65
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:17,860
when you try to apply it multiple times to generate

66
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:21,020
a long trajectory

67
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:21,910
Cool

68
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:23,660
Now, there's a second way that you can use

69
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:25,340
these kinds of learned models

70
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:28,420
And that is to learn a policy

71
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:31,940
And so that's what we'll talk about right now

72
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:33,820
And the key idea is that we can actually

73
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:38,100
use this model of the future, this kind of learned simulator

74
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,620
to augment any sort of data that we've collected

75
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:45,840
So say that in the real world or the real environment

76
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,460
we've collected some data

77
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:50,400
So we have some trajectories

78
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,780
Some of this data could also have been provided to us

79
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,340
in an offline data set

80
00:03:55,340 --> 00:04:01,130
And so maybe the data that we have in the real environment

81
00:04:01,130 --> 00:04:04,610
looks something like this

82
00:04:04,610 --> 00:04:11,130
Does anyone have thoughts on ways

83
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:15,970
in which we might want to use our learned model to augment

84
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:19,490
this data set right here

85
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:22,050
Like, procedurally, what would we

86
00:04:22,050 --> 00:04:25,150
do to try to generate synthetic data

87
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:25,650
Yeah

88
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:28,100
So the thought there was that you could take--

89
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,050
you said you could take the last state

90
00:04:30,050 --> 00:04:31,470
and then generate more from there

91
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:37,410
So you could add on to the last state here

92
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:39,010
So that's one thing you could do

93
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:40,590
Other thoughts on things you could do

94
00:04:40,590 --> 00:04:41,850
Yeah

95
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:44,890
So something like dagger, where you take the first part that's

96
00:04:44,890 --> 00:04:46,930
run by the policy and then the second part that's

97
00:04:46,930 --> 00:04:47,900
from the model

98
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:49,530
So it's kind of similar to this, maybe

99
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:50,910
But maybe you do it earlier

100
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:52,470
Is that what you're saying

101
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:52,970
Yeah

102
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:56,620
So you don't just have to start at the end

103
00:04:56,620 --> 00:04:59,580
You can also start anywhere in the middle as well

104
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:03,280
So you could, for example, generate trajectories

105
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:07,600
from, really, any sort of state in your buffer

106
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,040
in your data set

107
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,720
And then if you were to do something like that

108
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:17,360
then you would be able to generate

109
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:20,640
data that looks like this, where everything that is in red

110
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:27,080
is synthetic data or data that was imagined using your model

111
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:31,280
And then everything in black is the real data

112
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,120
from the environment

113
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:35,480
Now, one thing that we talked about before

114
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:40,810
is that our model is not very good to be--

115
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:42,600
like, you can't really trust it if you were

116
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,480
to make a really long rollout

117
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:47,600
And so one thing that you can do is keep

118
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:49,920
these trajectories very short

119
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:53,960
And so instead of having these be almost as

120
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:55,820
long as your original trajectories

121
00:05:55,820 --> 00:06:02,640
you could actually change them to be just little short rollouts

122
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:06,120
that look something like this and that basically

123
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:10,320
add a little bit more coverage around the trajectory

124
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:11,640
in your data

125
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:16,840
And so that would look something like this

126
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:20,680
And something like this picture is often more

127
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,280
like what people find works really

128
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:31,480
well in practice because of inaccuracies in your model

129
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,140
And so specifically-- say we have some example

130
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:36,880
real trajectory

131
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,840
Then maybe the first way that you could

132
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,960
think about creating data--

133
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,500
maybe it wasn't the first way that you guys thought about it

134
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:45,440
But one simple thing you could do

135
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:47,080
is just start from initial states

136
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:49,160
and generate full trajectories as if it

137
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:51,120
were the real environment

138
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:53,230
But the downside there is that the model may not

139
00:06:53,230 --> 00:06:56,830
be accurate for long horizons

140
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:59,270
And so you won't be able to actually get very good state

141
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:02,910
coverage if you only start at the initial state distribution

142
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:07,910
and don't actually end, because you only want

143
00:07:07,910 --> 00:07:09,920
to use fairly short rollouts

144
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:11,670
You can also generate partial trajectories

145
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:13,030
from the initial states

146
00:07:13,030 --> 00:07:15,450
But this won't give you good coverage of the later states

147
00:07:15,450 --> 00:07:20,830
And so one thing that gets the best of both worlds-- it

148
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:23,550
gets you good coverage and also allows

149
00:07:23,550 --> 00:07:25,350
you to use short trajectories-- is

150
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:28,110
to generate these partial trajectories

151
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:34,950
from all of the states in your real data set

152
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:39,230
So then what the full algorithm looks like is

153
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:47,910
you will first collect real data

154
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:51,580
We can call this Denv, before

155
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:59,140
And then we will want to update our model using the real data

156
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:06,660
so that our model is as accurate as it can be

157
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:12,280
And then we can generate data

158
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:20,260
And the way this generation process will look like

159
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:30,660
is first sample some S from your real data

160
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:38,260
And then generate a short trajectory starting from S

161
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:46,220
where you will roll out your policy or whatever exploration

162
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:54,730
policy you have, starting from S

163
00:08:54,730 --> 00:08:57,290
But then we'll be doing this rollout

164
00:08:57,290 --> 00:09:05,410
inside your learned model

165
00:09:05,410 --> 00:09:09,290
And so this will be inside the world that

166
00:09:09,290 --> 00:09:14,050
is instantiated from your learned model and not

167
00:09:14,050 --> 00:09:15,730
in the real environment

168
00:09:15,730 --> 00:09:22,130
This data will go into some other data set

169
00:09:22,130 --> 00:09:28,570
Maybe we can call it Dgen for D-generated data

170
00:09:28,570 --> 00:09:32,570
And then at the end, you can simply

171
00:09:32,570 --> 00:09:36,210
update your policy using both the real data

172
00:09:36,210 --> 00:09:38,810
and the generated data

173
00:09:38,810 --> 00:09:53,450
So update pi theta using Denv and D generated

174
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:56,170
And then repeat this process

175
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:56,970
Whoops

176
00:09:56,970 --> 00:10:02,990
I misspelled real

177
00:10:02,990 --> 00:10:04,410
So the question is, if you're only

178
00:10:04,410 --> 00:10:07,530
generating short trajectories, does that take longer

179
00:10:07,530 --> 00:10:11,170
to converge to some goal

180
00:10:11,170 --> 00:10:20,370
So you can think of it as kind of supplementing

181
00:10:20,370 --> 00:10:22,030
the data that you have so far

182
00:10:22,030 --> 00:10:28,650
And when you start from states that are closer to the goal

183
00:10:28,650 --> 00:10:32,710
from when you generate data there

184
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:34,690
then you'll be close to getting rewards

185
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:36,810
And you'll be able to update

186
00:10:36,810 --> 00:10:40,250
When you're further, this doesn't mean that--

187
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:42,150
this data won't necessarily reach the goal

188
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:44,830
But with reinforcement learning algorithms

189
00:10:44,830 --> 00:10:49,000
you'll be able to understand-- some of this data

190
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,840
might feed into states that are in the data

191
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:55,000
And when you use reinforcement learning

192
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,500
you can then learn that--

193
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:59,360
like, as an example, maybe you have a real trajectory

194
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:03,560
that takes a path that is maybe not the most direct path

195
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:06,200
And then you start learning a policy that knows

196
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:08,620
maybe I should shortcut this

197
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:10,280
Then your model, when you explore

198
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:13,160
that in your synthetic data, this

199
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:15,560
might actually help you learn a better policy

200
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:18,120
by using this kind of data

201
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:21,200
In terms of convergence speed or speed

202
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:24,560
in which you'll get a good policy

203
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:32,960
generally, the thing that we're often trying to minimize

204
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:36,340
is the amount of real data that we need right here

205
00:11:36,340 --> 00:11:40,200
And this will allow you to get a good policy with less real data

206
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:42,980
by using these red trajectories

207
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:43,480
Yeah

208
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:45,770
So the question is, how do we get rewards in this case

209
00:11:45,770 --> 00:11:49,030
So I haven't explicitly written it

210
00:11:49,030 --> 00:11:52,950
out here because sometimes you might

211
00:11:52,950 --> 00:11:58,230
know the form of the reward function

212
00:11:58,230 --> 00:12:01,150
Or you might have learned a reward function

213
00:12:01,150 --> 00:12:07,350
in the original RL problem, in which case you can use that

214
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:10,380
If you didn't learn a reward function

215
00:12:10,380 --> 00:12:11,750
and the real world just gives you

216
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:13,890
the rewards as part of this data

217
00:12:13,890 --> 00:12:16,670
then when you train this model, you'll

218
00:12:16,670 --> 00:12:20,030
need for either this model or a separate model

219
00:12:20,030 --> 00:12:24,190
to also learn a reward function with supervised learning

220
00:12:24,190 --> 00:12:26,270
So the question was about compute

221
00:12:26,270 --> 00:12:29,710
So this is going to be more compute intensive

222
00:12:29,710 --> 00:12:33,790
during training than if you were to not use this, especially

223
00:12:33,790 --> 00:12:37,030
because sometimes the models that you might be learning--

224
00:12:37,030 --> 00:12:40,170
in some cases, the models that you're learning

225
00:12:40,170 --> 00:12:43,950
usually rolling out through them, takes compute

226
00:12:43,950 --> 00:12:48,020
You're kind of trading data in the environment for compute

227
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:50,780
And one of the reasons why that trade-off is often favorable

228
00:12:50,780 --> 00:12:53,740
is because collecting online data in the real environment

229
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:55,940
might be unsafe, or it might just

230
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:58,580
be really expensive if you have to run experiments

231
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:02,140
on physical equipment

232
00:13:02,140 --> 00:13:05,260
Unlike planning, however, this is not more compute intensive

233
00:13:05,260 --> 00:13:06,200
at test time

234
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:07,820
Because you're using the synthetic data

235
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:09,320
to update a policy

236
00:13:09,320 --> 00:13:12,220
And then at test time, you're running that policy

237
00:13:12,220 --> 00:13:18,340
as your final behavior

238
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:19,700
Cool

239
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:23,740
So the algorithm on the slides, we collect data

240
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:24,920
We update our model

241
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:27,580
This model may also model rewards

242
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:30,660
by trying to mimic the data from the environment

243
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:37,700
Then we will collect these short rollouts by using our model

244
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:41,180
starting from states in the real data

245
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:43,740
And then we'll update the policy and, optionally

246
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:47,540
also update any sort of value function using

247
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:51,980
the aggregated data

248
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:56,020
One other thing to note about this

249
00:13:56,020 --> 00:14:00,060
is that it is compatible with a variety of reinforcement

250
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:01,080
learning algorithms

251
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:06,580
So step 4, you can use a variety of algorithms here

252
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:09,700
You can even use on-policy reinforcement

253
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:11,980
learning algorithms as well

254
00:14:11,980 --> 00:14:15,780
In particular, if you have an on-policy algorithm that

255
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:18,700
needs data from the exact pi theta

256
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,140
that you're taking a gradient step on

257
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:22,740
all that means is that pi theta has

258
00:14:22,740 --> 00:14:26,020
to be the policy that was collecting the synthetic data

259
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:31,780
through the model

260
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:32,580
Cool

261
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:33,080
Yeah

262
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:37,340
So with regard to this last update, here

263
00:14:37,340 --> 00:14:39,820
we're using both the data from the environment and the data

264
00:14:39,820 --> 00:14:41,020
from the model

265
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:43,770
There are actually also some policy updates

266
00:14:43,770 --> 00:14:45,890
that only use data from the model

267
00:14:45,890 --> 00:14:51,510
and find that that works fine as well

268
00:14:51,510 --> 00:14:53,250
So you can choose to use either both

269
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:56,430
or just use the data from the model

270
00:14:56,430 --> 00:14:56,930
Yeah

271
00:14:56,930 --> 00:14:59,210
So this only makes sense when you have an estimate

272
00:14:59,210 --> 00:15:00,450
of the reward function

273
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:02,370
And if you don't, then you won't be

274
00:15:02,370 --> 00:15:06,460
able to update your policy on the generated data

275
00:15:06,460 --> 00:15:08,130
So the question-- is the learning stable

276
00:15:08,130 --> 00:15:13,730
when you use the synthetic data

277
00:15:13,730 --> 00:15:15,970
Yeah, yeah

278
00:15:15,970 --> 00:15:18,370
So you may be in a scenario where you're

279
00:15:18,370 --> 00:15:19,830
adding a lot of synthetic data

280
00:15:19,830 --> 00:15:22,330
And so how much synthetic data you generate

281
00:15:22,330 --> 00:15:26,770
and how you balance that data in this last update

282
00:15:26,770 --> 00:15:29,810
might depend on how good you think your model is

283
00:15:29,810 --> 00:15:35,450
In terms of stability, I think that--

284
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:38,330
we'll talk about pros and cons on the next slide

285
00:15:38,330 --> 00:15:40,510
But one downside of these kinds of algorithms

286
00:15:40,510 --> 00:15:44,280
is, they do introduce additional hyperparameters because you

287
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:45,340
have to train this model

288
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:48,780
And if, for example, your model training, for whatever reason

289
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:52,880
it didn't do well, then you might end up in a scenario

290
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:56,800
where the algorithm performs more

291
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:59,000
poorly because those hyperparameters weren't

292
00:15:59,000 --> 00:15:59,900
well tuned

293
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:02,880
And so you can see issues there

294
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:13,120
But otherwise, if you have a fairly accurate model

295
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:18,400
you usually don't have issues otherwise with stability

296
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:20,800
So let's talk a little bit about when

297
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:23,500
to use this sort of synthetic data

298
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:25,820
and when to use model-based reinforcement learning

299
00:16:25,820 --> 00:16:28,720
So generally, the way to think about it

300
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:31,320
is that there's potentially really big

301
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:33,400
upsides with model-based reinforcement learning

302
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:36,800
and also potentially big downsides

303
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:42,030
The big upside is that if you can learn a pretty good model

304
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:46,710
then it is immensely useful to be able to predict the future

305
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:51,230
And you can get away with using far, far less real data

306
00:16:51,230 --> 00:16:55,190
by using the data that's generated

307
00:16:55,190 --> 00:16:59,630
And so in domains where it's feasible to learn a model

308
00:16:59,630 --> 00:17:00,830
it's really, really useful

309
00:17:00,830 --> 00:17:03,870
You could also do the kind of test time planning as well

310
00:17:03,870 --> 00:17:07,050
And generally, maybe one other point

311
00:17:07,050 --> 00:17:09,010
that's not on the slides with regard to upsides

312
00:17:09,010 --> 00:17:11,150
is that it seems like this notion of being

313
00:17:11,150 --> 00:17:15,240
able to anticipate future states as an outcome of your actions

314
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:16,990
that seems like a fairly fundamental thing

315
00:17:16,990 --> 00:17:23,190
that our models should at least have some understanding for

316
00:17:23,190 --> 00:17:25,270
One other thing that we actually didn't talk about

317
00:17:25,270 --> 00:17:28,550
is, there are also scenarios where the model can be

318
00:17:28,550 --> 00:17:30,430
trained without reward labels

319
00:17:30,430 --> 00:17:35,350
And, for example, if some of your real data has rewards

320
00:17:35,350 --> 00:17:37,510
and some of it doesn't have rewards

321
00:17:37,510 --> 00:17:39,130
the part that doesn't have rewards

322
00:17:39,130 --> 00:17:41,670
you can train your model on

323
00:17:41,670 --> 00:17:44,230
And your model can actually leverage potentially more data

324
00:17:44,230 --> 00:17:46,790
than what a model-free reinforcement learning

325
00:17:46,790 --> 00:17:49,830
method can use

326
00:17:49,830 --> 00:17:51,730
And so that's quite nice

327
00:17:51,730 --> 00:17:53,110
And there are scenarios where you

328
00:17:53,110 --> 00:17:55,230
might be able to collect very large amounts of data

329
00:17:55,230 --> 00:18:00,550
without rewards and a small amount of data with rewards

330
00:18:00,550 --> 00:18:03,070
And then the other thing is that the model is somewhat

331
00:18:03,070 --> 00:18:03,970
task agnostic

332
00:18:03,970 --> 00:18:06,690
And so if it was trained on a broad enough data distribution

333
00:18:06,690 --> 00:18:09,870
sometimes you can transfer this model across different tasks

334
00:18:09,870 --> 00:18:11,870
or across different rewards

335
00:18:11,870 --> 00:18:15,390
And so, for example, if your goal isn't just

336
00:18:15,390 --> 00:18:17,990
to learn one task but to learn many different tasks

337
00:18:17,990 --> 00:18:23,470
this sort of transferability is useful

338
00:18:23,470 --> 00:18:24,730
So those are the big upsides

339
00:18:24,730 --> 00:18:27,790
What about the big downsides

340
00:18:27,790 --> 00:18:30,710
One downside is that the model doesn't optimize for task

341
00:18:30,710 --> 00:18:34,230
performance and errors

342
00:18:34,230 --> 00:18:36,250
Because it's trying to-- for example

343
00:18:36,250 --> 00:18:39,640
if you have image observations or your states are images

344
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:43,660
then it's trying to get the details in the pixels to line up

345
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:47,980
and that, for example, the color of the sky is the correct color

346
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,300
And that, in some ways, oftentimes

347
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:53,220
is not actually what you care about in terms

348
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:54,940
of learning a good policy

349
00:18:54,940 --> 00:18:57,020
So there's this mismatch between what

350
00:18:57,020 --> 00:18:58,860
you're optimizing for when you train a model

351
00:18:58,860 --> 00:19:01,020
and what you actually might care about

352
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:05,780
in terms of downstream policy performance

353
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,960
Sometimes it's really hard to learn a good model

354
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,750
Sometimes it's harder to learn a good model

355
00:19:09,750 --> 00:19:11,300
than it is to learn a good policy

356
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:13,300
In these cases, usually, you don't

357
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:18,660
want to learn models because you're then presenting a harder

358
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:21,780
problem than the actual downstream problem you

359
00:19:21,780 --> 00:19:24,200
might care about

360
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:25,780
And then the last thing that I alluded

361
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:28,360
to before is that it's another thing you have to train

362
00:19:28,360 --> 00:19:30,060
And so there's more hyperparameters

363
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:32,300
and it's also more compute intensive

364
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:35,810
And so because of these big upsides and downsides

365
00:19:35,810 --> 00:19:39,010
generally, whether to try to learn

366
00:19:39,010 --> 00:19:42,890
this kind of predictive model depends a lot on the domain

367
00:19:42,890 --> 00:19:46,590
and how hard it is to learn that kind of model

368
00:19:46,590 --> 00:19:47,090
Yeah

369
00:19:47,090 --> 00:19:48,920
So the question was, what are a few examples

370
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:50,770
of the additional hyperparameters

371
00:19:50,770 --> 00:19:55,170
The main thing all comes down to this step 2 right here

372
00:19:55,170 --> 00:19:59,010
which is that you need to design the architecture of the model

373
00:19:59,010 --> 00:20:00,450
of this predictive model

374
00:20:00,450 --> 00:20:02,370
And it might need to be a completely different

375
00:20:02,370 --> 00:20:03,950
architecture from the policy if you're

376
00:20:03,950 --> 00:20:06,890
going to be generating images or states

377
00:20:06,890 --> 00:20:10,200
The second is that you need a learning rate

378
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:12,030
You need to know how long to train that for

379
00:20:12,030 --> 00:20:17,530
like how many epochs, any regularization for that model

380
00:20:17,530 --> 00:20:20,410
basically everything that might go into training

381
00:20:20,410 --> 00:20:22,330
a model like this

382
00:20:22,330 --> 00:20:24,870
One more thing on that previous question--

383
00:20:24,870 --> 00:20:26,330
beyond training this model, there's

384
00:20:26,330 --> 00:20:29,730
also hyperparameters with regard to the length of these rollouts

385
00:20:29,730 --> 00:20:33,090
and how much synthetic data to generate

386
00:20:33,090 --> 00:20:36,330
So the question is, do we keep on collecting data

387
00:20:36,330 --> 00:20:37,830
after each policy update

388
00:20:37,830 --> 00:20:43,670
So as written, this is an online algorithm

389
00:20:43,670 --> 00:20:47,410
You can actually also develop completely offline versions

390
00:20:47,410 --> 00:20:49,170
model-based RL versions as well, where

391
00:20:49,170 --> 00:20:52,610
you have a fixed prior data set and you augment

392
00:20:52,610 --> 00:20:54,270
that data set with a model

393
00:20:54,270 --> 00:20:57,340
And then you train a policy, and you're done

394
00:20:57,340 --> 00:20:59,410
In this case, we have an online algorithm

395
00:20:59,410 --> 00:21:02,190
where we then continue to collect real data

396
00:21:02,190 --> 00:21:07,230
update our model, and so forth

397
00:21:07,230 --> 00:21:09,090
This can be done in an off-policy way

398
00:21:09,090 --> 00:21:10,370
or an on-policy way

399
00:21:10,370 --> 00:21:14,150
So that's going to come down to, when you do this update

400
00:21:14,150 --> 00:21:19,250
is it going to only use the Denv and the Dgen from this iteration

401
00:21:19,250 --> 00:21:21,890
or also from previous iterations

402
00:21:21,890 --> 00:21:24,490
And that's going to depend on what algorithm

403
00:21:24,490 --> 00:21:26,810
you use to update your policy

404
00:21:26,810 --> 00:21:28,430
So generally, models are very flexible

405
00:21:28,430 --> 00:21:31,450
You can use them offline, on policy

406
00:21:31,450 --> 00:21:33,300
off policy, and so forth

407
00:21:33,300 --> 00:21:33,800
Yeah

408
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:37,280
So the question is, how hard is it

409
00:21:37,280 --> 00:21:40,200
to learn a model compared to a policy

410
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:41,980
It's extremely domain dependent

411
00:21:41,980 --> 00:21:48,960
So for example, say you're in a maze

412
00:21:48,960 --> 00:21:53,980
So you're trying to learn how to get from here to here

413
00:21:53,980 --> 00:21:56,000
Maybe there's some walls

414
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:00,520
And you need to figure out how to get

415
00:22:00,520 --> 00:22:04,000
through this sort of environment

416
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,080
And you're not able to go through the walls

417
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:09,180
In this case, the model might be quite easy to learn

418
00:22:09,180 --> 00:22:11,780
You'll learn that if your action is to go right

419
00:22:11,780 --> 00:22:13,860
then you'll go right, unless there's a wall there

420
00:22:13,860 --> 00:22:16,920
If you're going to go up and so forth-- that's really easy

421
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:18,800
But the policy is really complicated

422
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:21,720
Because the policy is saying that, OK, if you're here

423
00:22:21,720 --> 00:22:24,480
maybe you need to go in various different directions

424
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:28,840
And so this is a case where the policy is a lot more complicated

425
00:22:28,840 --> 00:22:29,820
than the model

426
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:32,630
And learning a model could be a good choice

427
00:22:32,630 --> 00:22:37,110
As another example, say that you're

428
00:22:37,110 --> 00:22:40,790
trying to pour some water from one cup

429
00:22:40,790 --> 00:22:44,070
into a bowl or something like that

430
00:22:44,070 --> 00:22:46,610
The policy might be potentially fairly simple

431
00:22:46,610 --> 00:22:52,510
Like, maybe you're already even holding the cup in your hand

432
00:22:52,510 --> 00:22:54,430
Then the policy might be as simple as just

433
00:22:54,430 --> 00:22:56,750
rotating your wrist

434
00:22:56,750 --> 00:22:58,710
But the model in this sort of case

435
00:22:58,710 --> 00:23:02,710
might involve the fluid dynamics of how the water will pour

436
00:23:02,710 --> 00:23:05,430
and what will happen, for example, if it hits

437
00:23:05,430 --> 00:23:07,590
the edge of the bowl or the center of the bowl

438
00:23:07,590 --> 00:23:08,850
or something like that

439
00:23:08,850 --> 00:23:12,230
And this is a scenario where the model is a lot more complicated

440
00:23:12,230 --> 00:23:13,750
than the policy

441
00:23:13,750 --> 00:23:17,830
And it's possible that in this scenario

442
00:23:17,830 --> 00:23:19,870
it might be a lot easier to just learn the policy

443
00:23:19,870 --> 00:23:21,270
without learning the model

444
00:23:21,270 --> 00:23:23,210
So it really depends on the domain

445
00:23:23,210 --> 00:23:23,710
Yeah

446
00:23:23,710 --> 00:23:26,150
So the question is, how successful has model-based RL

447
00:23:26,150 --> 00:23:32,300
been in robotics scenarios

448
00:23:32,300 --> 00:23:34,340
If you're dealing with high-dimensional image

449
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:38,060
observations, it's arguably been less successful

450
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:41,420
If you have a more compact state space

451
00:23:41,420 --> 00:23:44,480
like the positions of joints and positions of objects

452
00:23:44,480 --> 00:23:46,940
then it's been a lot more successful

453
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:51,960
And with legged robots, for example

454
00:23:51,960 --> 00:23:56,600
you can use model-based RL pretty successfully

455
00:23:56,600 --> 00:23:59,100
if you're mostly just trying to learn, like, a blind walking

456
00:23:59,100 --> 00:24:00,180
policy

457
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:03,620
Or we saw the dexterous-- like, the hand manipulation

458
00:24:03,620 --> 00:24:06,460
where you knew the position of the two balls

459
00:24:06,460 --> 00:24:08,480
It was also very successful for that

460
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:12,500
And I haven't tried to do model-free RL for that problem

461
00:24:12,500 --> 00:24:15,710
Or even imitation learning would be very, very challenging

462
00:24:15,710 --> 00:24:17,540
And so it's been extremely successful there

463
00:24:17,540 --> 00:24:20,220
In scenarios where you need to have image observations

464
00:24:20,220 --> 00:24:21,620
it gets a lot harder

465
00:24:21,620 --> 00:24:24,240
It doesn't mean that--

466
00:24:24,240 --> 00:24:26,280
video-generative models are getting a lot better

467
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:31,660
And so I could see a future where this sort of approach

468
00:24:31,660 --> 00:24:35,960
might also be quite successful in the future

469
00:24:35,960 --> 00:24:41,300
But right now, it hasn't been super successful

470
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:43,420
The last thing I'll mention on model-based RL

471
00:24:43,420 --> 00:24:47,660
is that so far, we've been modeling this predictive model

472
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:49,140
of the future

473
00:24:49,140 --> 00:24:51,020
And there's actually a lot of other objects

474
00:24:51,020 --> 00:24:52,700
that you could learn

475
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:56,400
So you could learn what's called an inverse model

476
00:24:56,400 --> 00:25:01,430
You could learn, for two states--

477
00:25:01,430 --> 00:25:04,400
like, if I'm currently in state st

478
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:08,290
what is the action that would lead to state st plus 1

479
00:25:08,290 --> 00:25:10,040
You could learn a multistep inverse model

480
00:25:10,040 --> 00:25:15,420
which is, if I'm going to end up at state st plus n

481
00:25:15,420 --> 00:25:16,800
what action would lead me there

482
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,260
Or what are the sequence of actions

483
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:20,860
that would lead me there

484
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:23,500
Both of these, in some ways, look closer to a policy

485
00:25:23,500 --> 00:25:28,570
except you're passing as input some future state rather than

486
00:25:28,570 --> 00:25:30,570
just the current state

487
00:25:30,570 --> 00:25:32,450
These are also things that can be learned

488
00:25:32,450 --> 00:25:34,310
without data with rewards

489
00:25:34,310 --> 00:25:36,730
You're kind of just learning about the environment and not

490
00:25:36,730 --> 00:25:40,410
about the particular task at hand

491
00:25:40,410 --> 00:25:42,390
You can predict futures without actions

492
00:25:42,390 --> 00:25:45,210
And that can think about, what are the affordances

493
00:25:45,210 --> 00:25:46,070
in my environment

494
00:25:46,070 --> 00:25:48,650
What are the things that could be

495
00:25:48,650 --> 00:25:50,270
possible to happen in the future

496
00:25:50,270 --> 00:25:53,250
irrespective of the actions that I take

497
00:25:53,250 --> 00:25:59,490
You could try to interpolate between two states

498
00:25:59,490 --> 00:26:03,900
You could learn a generative model of transitions of st

499
00:26:03,900 --> 00:26:05,250
at st plus 1

500
00:26:05,250 --> 00:26:10,050
There's some cool properties with that sort of model

501
00:26:10,050 --> 00:26:14,370
And these kind of models can be pretty useful too

502
00:26:14,370 --> 00:26:16,230
They have various purposes

503
00:26:16,230 --> 00:26:18,690
There's lots of research on using different kinds of models

504
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:19,970
like these

505
00:26:19,970 --> 00:26:21,690
And so it's just something to think

506
00:26:21,690 --> 00:26:24,370
about in terms of in some domains you could imagine

507
00:26:24,370 --> 00:26:27,960
Or with some data sources, you could imagine scenarios

508
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:29,980
where these might be useful

509
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:30,480
Yeah

510
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:32,640
So the question is, if you use data

511
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:35,580
that doesn't have rewards as part of your model training

512
00:26:35,580 --> 00:26:37,800
is there going to be a mismatch between the accuracy

513
00:26:37,800 --> 00:26:41,800
of predicting next states and predicting rewards

514
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:47,160
There certainly could be a mismatch like that

515
00:26:47,160 --> 00:26:50,720
Generally, rewards are a bit easier to learn

516
00:26:50,720 --> 00:26:53,240
how to predict than future states

517
00:26:53,240 --> 00:26:58,490
And so even if you have a lot more data

518
00:26:58,490 --> 00:27:00,240
for learning the model, you could actually

519
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:01,740
end up in a scenario where the model

520
00:27:01,740 --> 00:27:05,240
is less accurate than the estimated reward

521
00:27:05,240 --> 00:27:07,320
But yeah, you can definitely end up with scenarios

522
00:27:07,320 --> 00:27:09,040
where the reward is less accurate

523
00:27:09,040 --> 00:27:12,000
and where you need to specifically gather

524
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,180
data for that

525
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:17,400
Yeah

526
00:27:17,400 --> 00:27:17,900
Cool

527
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:18,400
OK

528
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:21,680
So that's it for model-based RL

529
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:25,710
Now we're going to talk a bit about multitask learning

530
00:27:25,710 --> 00:27:28,710
and what that looks like and also different techniques

531
00:27:28,710 --> 00:27:33,710
that we can do, beyond a fairly naive and simple technique

532
00:27:33,710 --> 00:27:38,790
So what is multitask reinforcement learning

533
00:27:38,790 --> 00:27:44,190
The key idea is that we want to be able to learn a generalist

534
00:27:44,190 --> 00:27:47,830
policy that isn't just aiming to do one thing

535
00:27:47,830 --> 00:27:52,270
like being able to pour water, for example

536
00:27:52,270 --> 00:27:56,350
but that can do many different things

537
00:27:56,350 --> 00:27:58,510
The examples of this--

538
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:01,190
we might want a generalist language model assistant

539
00:28:01,190 --> 00:28:02,950
that can book travel for us, that

540
00:28:02,950 --> 00:28:05,830
can do online grocery shopping

541
00:28:05,830 --> 00:28:08,490
We might want a legged robot that can walk, that can run

542
00:28:08,490 --> 00:28:12,110
that can dance, that can crouch, maybe a mobile robot that

543
00:28:12,110 --> 00:28:16,550
can hang up a towel, unload a dishwasher, wipe a spill

544
00:28:16,550 --> 00:28:19,710
maybe want a music recommender system that can personalize

545
00:28:19,710 --> 00:28:23,270
recommendations for one person and also

546
00:28:23,270 --> 00:28:25,070
be able to personalize recommendations

547
00:28:25,070 --> 00:28:27,350
to a different person

548
00:28:27,350 --> 00:28:29,750
Or we might want something that can

549
00:28:29,750 --> 00:28:31,550
play lots of different games, like being

550
00:28:31,550 --> 00:28:35,790
able to play Pokemon and pinball and Flappy Bird and so forth

551
00:28:35,790 --> 00:28:38,070
And there are scenarios where if you

552
00:28:38,070 --> 00:28:41,590
think about each of these tasks as their own reinforcement

553
00:28:41,590 --> 00:28:44,550
learning problem, they might have different reward

554
00:28:44,550 --> 00:28:46,510
functions from other tasks

555
00:28:46,510 --> 00:28:49,410
They might, in some cases, have different dynamics

556
00:28:49,410 --> 00:28:51,750
like the dynamics of different games

557
00:28:51,750 --> 00:28:54,030
or the dynamics of different people

558
00:28:54,030 --> 00:28:58,390
They might have different action spaces, even

559
00:28:58,390 --> 00:29:02,510
And so when you're trying to train policies

560
00:29:02,510 --> 00:29:04,270
across these different tasks, there's

561
00:29:04,270 --> 00:29:11,830
some considerations that come up when you try to do that

562
00:29:11,830 --> 00:29:16,370
Now, the key idea that we'll see is that you can, in many cases

563
00:29:16,370 --> 00:29:18,110
just go about normal reinforcement

564
00:29:18,110 --> 00:29:20,110
learning but condition on the task

565
00:29:20,110 --> 00:29:21,830
that you're trying to learn

566
00:29:21,830 --> 00:29:27,100
So you're then going to be learning a policy that is not

567
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:29,540
just conditioned on the state, but It's

568
00:29:29,540 --> 00:29:31,930
conditioned on the state and also the task

569
00:29:31,930 --> 00:29:36,740
that you want it to be performing for that state

570
00:29:36,740 --> 00:29:39,660
Now, why do we care about multitask reinforcement

571
00:29:39,660 --> 00:29:40,740
learning

572
00:29:40,740 --> 00:29:44,880
So if we go back to a lot of the topics

573
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:46,420
that we've talked about in the course

574
00:29:46,420 --> 00:29:50,100
so far, like imitation learning, on-policy and off-policy RL

575
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:53,060
offline RL, model-based RL, reward functions

576
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:57,730
a big topic that's recurred in many of these lectures

577
00:29:57,730 --> 00:29:59,110
is talking about data efficiency

578
00:29:59,110 --> 00:30:03,820
Like, how much data do we need to be able to calculate

579
00:30:03,820 --> 00:30:07,140
a gradient that is accurate

580
00:30:07,140 --> 00:30:11,620
And if our ultimate goal is to train something

581
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:14,620
that can do multiple tasks at once

582
00:30:14,620 --> 00:30:16,980
then we can actually amortize the complexity

583
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:20,100
of learning any one task by actually

584
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:24,370
training that policy across tasks and across scenarios

585
00:30:24,370 --> 00:30:28,370
So it could actually be a boost for data efficiency in the sense

586
00:30:28,370 --> 00:30:32,570
that the data efficiency for training any one task

587
00:30:32,570 --> 00:30:35,010
will be lower when you're actually trying

588
00:30:35,010 --> 00:30:37,050
to train everything together

589
00:30:37,050 --> 00:30:40,910
And that's because tasks typically

590
00:30:40,910 --> 00:30:43,410
will have some sort of shared structure

591
00:30:43,410 --> 00:30:46,950
if you're trying to be a language model assistant

592
00:30:46,950 --> 00:30:49,530
there is going to be a shared structure with regard to grammar

593
00:30:49,530 --> 00:30:52,170
or maybe with regard to different websites

594
00:30:52,170 --> 00:30:54,330
And if you learn how to interact with one website

595
00:30:54,330 --> 00:30:56,410
that can teach you something about how to interact

596
00:30:56,410 --> 00:30:58,270
with other websites

597
00:30:58,270 --> 00:30:59,730
Or with legged robots, if your goal

598
00:30:59,730 --> 00:31:02,290
is to be able to walk forward and walk backward

599
00:31:02,290 --> 00:31:06,210
then this shared notion of staying balanced

600
00:31:06,210 --> 00:31:09,290
will be shared across tasks, among other things

601
00:31:09,290 --> 00:31:13,250
Or if you're trying to develop a recommender system that's

602
00:31:13,250 --> 00:31:15,570
personalized to different users, then some users

603
00:31:15,570 --> 00:31:17,140
will share a lot of similarities

604
00:31:17,140 --> 00:31:19,600
where if you try to learn to be a great recommender for one

605
00:31:19,600 --> 00:31:23,560
person, then there are other people that you could also

606
00:31:23,560 --> 00:31:27,040
probably be a recommender for

607
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:30,200
And then there's also a slightly deeper motivation as well

608
00:31:30,200 --> 00:31:33,560
which is that when we actually try

609
00:31:33,560 --> 00:31:36,280
to change these general systems rather than systems that

610
00:31:36,280 --> 00:31:40,600
do one task, we often find that generalist systems end up

611
00:31:40,600 --> 00:31:43,240
being more reliable and more performant than if we

612
00:31:43,240 --> 00:31:46,840
were to just train on one task

613
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:50,240
And so I think that this is a pretty cool finding that

614
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:54,320
has empirically been true in recent years in AI

615
00:31:54,320 --> 00:31:57,160
And so it's extra motivation for not just training

616
00:31:57,160 --> 00:32:01,880
something for one particular task or problem or user

617
00:32:01,880 --> 00:32:04,260
Let's maybe get into what this actually entails

618
00:32:04,260 --> 00:32:06,600
And we actually need to think about

619
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:09,640
how do we think about what a task is

620
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:12,480
It's actually, in some ways, not very well defined

621
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:14,480
what makes something a different task versus not

622
00:32:14,480 --> 00:32:15,920
a different task

623
00:32:15,920 --> 00:32:19,480
But one way to think about it is having different tasks just

624
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:22,560
be different RL problems, different Markov decision

625
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:24,040
processes

626
00:32:24,040 --> 00:32:28,960
And so each task will have a state space, action space

627
00:32:28,960 --> 00:32:33,960
an initial state distribution, a transition model, and a reward

628
00:32:33,960 --> 00:32:36,200
function

629
00:32:36,200 --> 00:32:39,480
And I'm indexing these with index i

630
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:42,340
to denote that this is the state space for task i

631
00:32:42,340 --> 00:32:45,120
This is the action space for task i

632
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:51,680
the dynamics and reward for task i, and so forth

633
00:32:51,680 --> 00:32:54,600
Now, with this definition, it's worth

634
00:32:54,600 --> 00:33:00,320
mentioning that when we think of the notion of a task

635
00:33:00,320 --> 00:33:04,120
there's a meaning under the English word of task

636
00:33:04,120 --> 00:33:07,440
And this notion of a task being an MDP

637
00:33:07,440 --> 00:33:09,280
is actually potentially quite different

638
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:12,440
from that semantic notion of a task

639
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:14,810
where you could actually have different tasks that

640
00:33:14,810 --> 00:33:16,520
have potentially the same reward function

641
00:33:16,520 --> 00:33:20,150
and only have different dynamics

642
00:33:20,150 --> 00:33:25,430
For example, if you're trying to walk forward

643
00:33:25,430 --> 00:33:29,510
on grass versus trying to walk forward on flat terrain

644
00:33:29,510 --> 00:33:33,150
then that potentially could be considered two different tasks

645
00:33:33,150 --> 00:33:35,070
under this framework, even though they're both

646
00:33:35,070 --> 00:33:37,710
the task of walking forward

647
00:33:37,710 --> 00:33:43,470
OK, so a few examples of multitask problems that we might

648
00:33:43,470 --> 00:33:44,950
think about

649
00:33:44,950 --> 00:33:48,470
One is this personalized recommendation problem

650
00:33:48,470 --> 00:33:49,630
that I mentioned

651
00:33:49,630 --> 00:33:53,190
In this case, different tasks would be different users

652
00:33:53,190 --> 00:33:54,870
or different people

653
00:33:54,870 --> 00:33:57,910
And different people might have different reward functions

654
00:33:57,910 --> 00:34:00,170
Like, if it's a music recommendation

655
00:34:00,170 --> 00:34:02,290
someone might really love rock music

656
00:34:02,290 --> 00:34:06,590
Another person really might love hip-hop music

657
00:34:06,590 --> 00:34:15,900
And also, the dynamics with regard to how often they even

658
00:34:15,900 --> 00:34:21,739
listen to music and so forth might also change across users

659
00:34:21,739 --> 00:34:24,940
Reinforcement learning-- this is an application we haven't really

660
00:34:24,940 --> 00:34:28,219
talked about, but it's often used in character animation

661
00:34:28,219 --> 00:34:31,139
in order to actually create animations

662
00:34:31,139 --> 00:34:34,060
of different kinds of characters

663
00:34:34,060 --> 00:34:39,219
like people or other animated characters that

664
00:34:39,219 --> 00:34:41,100
appear in movies

665
00:34:41,100 --> 00:34:44,860
And you might want to allow and be

666
00:34:44,860 --> 00:34:47,659
able to animate a character to do lots of different maneuvers

667
00:34:47,659 --> 00:34:50,219
So you might want to be able to have your character be

668
00:34:50,219 --> 00:34:52,139
able to do a backflip or to be able to run

669
00:34:52,139 --> 00:34:54,060
or be able to do a cartwheel

670
00:34:54,060 --> 00:34:58,020
And each of these would be different tasks

671
00:34:58,020 --> 00:35:00,420
Does anyone have thoughts for these tasks

672
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:02,580
What is the thing that would vary across tasks

673
00:35:02,580 --> 00:35:05,080
versus be fixed across tasks

674
00:35:05,080 --> 00:35:05,580
Yeah

675
00:35:05,580 --> 00:35:09,140
So in this scenario, the reward will vary because in one case

676
00:35:09,140 --> 00:35:10,120
you want it to run

677
00:35:10,120 --> 00:35:12,620
In another case, you want it to do a cartwheel

678
00:35:12,620 --> 00:35:17,540
And the dynamics or the physics of the simulator

679
00:35:17,540 --> 00:35:21,020
will stay the same

680
00:35:21,020 --> 00:35:21,900
Cool

681
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:23,860
There's also been works that's trying

682
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:29,240
to do character animation involving different garments

683
00:35:29,240 --> 00:35:32,340
so like a character that can put on a shirt versus a long sleeve

684
00:35:32,340 --> 00:35:33,930
shirt versus a dress

685
00:35:33,930 --> 00:35:35,300
Does anyone have thoughts on what

686
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:37,700
would vary across these tasks

687
00:35:37,700 --> 00:35:38,200
Yeah

688
00:35:38,200 --> 00:35:39,080
So the policy might vary

689
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:40,580
The policy will also vary, actually

690
00:35:40,580 --> 00:35:43,960
in all of these cases for different tasks

691
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:44,460
Yeah

692
00:35:44,460 --> 00:35:47,980
So the physics of how a long-sleeve shirt

693
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:51,660
versus a short-sleeve shirt will behave will vary across tasks

694
00:35:51,660 --> 00:35:54,340
I think I also put the initial state distribution might also

695
00:35:54,340 --> 00:35:57,260
vary, whereas in the first example

696
00:35:57,260 --> 00:35:58,660
maybe the initial state is always

697
00:35:58,660 --> 00:36:01,620
to, say, start standing up, for example

698
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:05,620
whereas here, you have different garments in the initial state

699
00:36:05,620 --> 00:36:08,060
And then the last example I put is, maybe

700
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:11,020
different tasks are controlling different robot arms

701
00:36:11,020 --> 00:36:14,170
And maybe one robot arm looks like this with maybe 4

702
00:36:14,170 --> 00:36:15,090
degrees of freedom

703
00:36:15,090 --> 00:36:16,510
This one has 6 degrees of freedom

704
00:36:16,510 --> 00:36:18,850
This one has 7 degrees of freedom

705
00:36:18,850 --> 00:36:21,010
Does anyone have thoughts on what

706
00:36:21,010 --> 00:36:25,890
would vary across tasks if one task is

707
00:36:25,890 --> 00:36:28,030
to control the first robot to grasp an object

708
00:36:28,030 --> 00:36:30,770
the second task is to control the second robot to grasp

709
00:36:30,770 --> 00:36:32,510
an object, and so forth

710
00:36:32,510 --> 00:36:33,010
Yeah

711
00:36:33,010 --> 00:36:35,090
So the actions themselves--

712
00:36:35,090 --> 00:36:37,590
so you'll have different dimensions of actions

713
00:36:37,590 --> 00:36:38,090
Yeah

714
00:36:38,090 --> 00:36:39,570
So the dynamics will also be different

715
00:36:39,570 --> 00:36:41,250
Because when you move one joint, that

716
00:36:41,250 --> 00:36:44,150
will affect the other joints differently

717
00:36:44,150 --> 00:36:44,650
Yeah

718
00:36:44,650 --> 00:36:46,670
So the states would also vary as well

719
00:36:46,670 --> 00:36:52,450
So how you represent the arm, that

720
00:36:52,450 --> 00:36:54,170
will be a different dimension

721
00:36:54,170 --> 00:36:57,950
What the actions even are will also be different dimensional

722
00:36:57,950 --> 00:37:00,710
And then the dynamics will also vary quite a bit as well

723
00:37:00,710 --> 00:37:03,130
because how your actions affect the robot

724
00:37:03,130 --> 00:37:06,690
arm are going to change

725
00:37:06,690 --> 00:37:08,010
Cool

726
00:37:08,010 --> 00:37:10,370
So those are examples of different problems

727
00:37:10,370 --> 00:37:16,280
where we might want to be training different tasks

728
00:37:16,280 --> 00:37:19,040
The last thing that we're missing here

729
00:37:19,040 --> 00:37:21,160
is, we need to figure out how to identify

730
00:37:21,160 --> 00:37:24,080
one task from another task so that we

731
00:37:24,080 --> 00:37:27,240
can tell our policy what task it should

732
00:37:27,240 --> 00:37:32,760
be doing at a given point

733
00:37:32,760 --> 00:37:35,540
And one way to do this is to just assign an index

734
00:37:35,540 --> 00:37:37,340
You could say this robot is task 0

735
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:38,460
This robot is task 1

736
00:37:38,460 --> 00:37:42,800
This robot is task 2 and so forth

737
00:37:42,800 --> 00:37:46,580
If you don't have any other better way to identify a task

738
00:37:46,580 --> 00:37:50,280
this is kind of what you resort to

739
00:37:50,280 --> 00:37:53,600
You could also have a language description of the task

740
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:55,560
You could have a video illustration

741
00:37:55,560 --> 00:37:57,140
of what the task is

742
00:37:57,140 --> 00:37:58,520
There's actually a number of ways

743
00:37:58,520 --> 00:38:02,800
to be creative with how you describe the task to the policy

744
00:38:02,800 --> 00:38:07,340
And we'll refer to each of these as different task identifiers

745
00:38:07,340 --> 00:38:12,310
So the task identifier for the task of--

746
00:38:12,310 --> 00:38:17,950
well, actually, we'll see some examples on the next slide

747
00:38:17,950 --> 00:38:20,430
Now, once you have the task identifier

748
00:38:20,430 --> 00:38:23,870
an alternative view on the multitask RL problem

749
00:38:23,870 --> 00:38:27,270
is simply just having the task identifier

750
00:38:27,270 --> 00:38:30,030
be part of the state, where we're writing

751
00:38:30,030 --> 00:38:32,830
s bar as the original state

752
00:38:32,830 --> 00:38:35,430
And what this means is that before

753
00:38:35,430 --> 00:38:40,710
we had a kind of a collection of MDPs

754
00:38:40,710 --> 00:38:43,190
That's what's written at the top

755
00:38:43,190 --> 00:38:54,430
And we can construct a single MDP that actually

756
00:38:54,430 --> 00:39:00,910
covers all of the tasks

757
00:39:00,910 --> 00:39:07,510
We have, for example, the Pi of the initial state

758
00:39:07,510 --> 00:39:15,230
Pi of s prime, given s, a and ri of s, a

759
00:39:15,230 --> 00:39:20,590
And this is like the dynamics and the reward for each task

760
00:39:20,590 --> 00:39:24,630
And we can think of it as like, our goal

761
00:39:24,630 --> 00:39:28,870
is to solve all of the MDPs for each of the tasks

762
00:39:28,870 --> 00:39:33,110
But we can also think about it as just a single MDP, where

763
00:39:33,110 --> 00:39:39,310
the state is now defined as like, maybe this

764
00:39:39,310 --> 00:39:45,310
is the original state space of these tasks

765
00:39:45,310 --> 00:39:48,750
And the state is now defined as the original state

766
00:39:48,750 --> 00:39:55,630
space and the task identifier for that task

767
00:39:55,630 --> 00:39:58,390
And then the reward function, for example

768
00:39:58,390 --> 00:40:00,870
in this modified MDP--

769
00:40:00,870 --> 00:40:03,190
that's just a single MDP for all the tasks--

770
00:40:03,190 --> 00:40:13,580
is then going to be equal to-- let's say, just to be explicit

771
00:40:13,580 --> 00:40:21,180
the reward for this state and this action

772
00:40:21,180 --> 00:40:29,260
would then be equal to ri of s bar a

773
00:40:29,260 --> 00:40:33,140
And similarly, you can write something out for the dynamics

774
00:40:33,140 --> 00:40:44,900
as well, where the aggregate MDP for a given task

775
00:40:44,900 --> 00:40:55,020
s prime given s bar zi comma a is equal to the kind of dynamics

776
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:58,680
for that task

777
00:40:58,680 --> 00:41:00,300
This gets a little bit more complicated

778
00:41:00,300 --> 00:41:04,260
if your tasks have different state spaces and action spaces

779
00:41:04,260 --> 00:41:07,310
And so if they have, for example

780
00:41:07,310 --> 00:41:08,970
different dimensionalities, then you

781
00:41:08,970 --> 00:41:11,650
need to think about how you would represent this state

782
00:41:11,650 --> 00:41:13,730
space in the MDP

783
00:41:13,730 --> 00:41:16,010
If they have the same state space and action space

784
00:41:16,010 --> 00:41:19,410
you can basically construct this aggregated Markov decision

785
00:41:19,410 --> 00:41:24,050
process with these equations here

786
00:41:24,050 --> 00:41:32,110
Now, if we go back to what we think of as the single task

787
00:41:32,110 --> 00:41:34,690
reinforcement learning setting that we've talked about so far

788
00:41:34,690 --> 00:41:37,570
we can then think about multitask RL

789
00:41:37,570 --> 00:41:41,430
but where the task identifier is part of the state, where

790
00:41:41,430 --> 00:41:43,010
the task identifier could be the task

791
00:41:43,010 --> 00:41:45,850
like an index, a language description

792
00:41:45,850 --> 00:41:48,010
It could also be some desired goal state

793
00:41:48,010 --> 00:41:50,570
as well that you want to reach

794
00:41:50,570 --> 00:41:53,610
This is often referred to as goal-conditioned RL

795
00:41:53,610 --> 00:41:55,130
But then once you do this, you're

796
00:41:55,130 --> 00:42:03,650
basically getting a modified MDP that covers each of these tasks

797
00:42:03,650 --> 00:42:05,880
Now, in terms of the reward, like we talked about

798
00:42:05,880 --> 00:42:10,760
on the board, it's basically going to be the same as before

799
00:42:10,760 --> 00:42:13,160
In this special case of goal-conditioned reinforcement

800
00:42:13,160 --> 00:42:17,560
learning, where you're trying to reach a goal, then oftentimes

801
00:42:17,560 --> 00:42:22,280
you'll think about the reward as some negative distance

802
00:42:22,280 --> 00:42:31,230
between the state that you're currently at and the goal state

803
00:42:31,230 --> 00:42:32,900
And distance functions can be Euclidean

804
00:42:32,900 --> 00:42:36,060
It could be a sparse 0, 1 and so forth

805
00:42:36,060 --> 00:42:39,960
So we still get kind of a standard Markov decision process

806
00:42:39,960 --> 00:42:43,800
when we bring these tasks together

807
00:42:43,800 --> 00:42:47,520
And you might ask, if we can get this standard Markov decision

808
00:42:47,520 --> 00:42:50,440
process that now has a task identifier, like a task string

809
00:42:50,440 --> 00:42:53,680
as part of the state space, then why not

810
00:42:53,680 --> 00:42:58,440
apply standard reinforcement learning algorithms to them

811
00:42:58,440 --> 00:43:01,080
And the answer is, you can

812
00:43:01,080 --> 00:43:04,320
And in many cases, this is a great way

813
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:10,210
to approach scenarios where you want to learn multiple tasks

814
00:43:10,210 --> 00:43:12,000
But the reason why I'm bringing all this up

815
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:13,760
is that in some cases, you can actually

816
00:43:13,760 --> 00:43:18,400
do better by leveraging the known structure

817
00:43:18,400 --> 00:43:20,560
between different tasks

818
00:43:20,560 --> 00:43:22,480
And goal-conditioned RL is one of those cases

819
00:43:22,480 --> 00:43:24,840
where you're trying to reach different goals

820
00:43:24,840 --> 00:43:27,280
And in the next few slides, we'll

821
00:43:27,280 --> 00:43:29,960
talk about one particular way that you

822
00:43:29,960 --> 00:43:33,640
can do a lot better by leveraging this fact

823
00:43:33,640 --> 00:43:37,520
that this MDP consists of these different tasks

824
00:43:37,520 --> 00:43:39,100
that you're trying to do

825
00:43:39,100 --> 00:43:39,600
Yeah

826
00:43:39,600 --> 00:43:41,500
Euclidean L2, that's just mean squared error

827
00:43:41,500 --> 00:43:42,400
Yeah

828
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:45,740
So the question is, if different people are different tasks

829
00:43:45,740 --> 00:43:49,400
then is there a better way to represent different people

830
00:43:49,400 --> 00:43:51,440
than a one hot--

831
00:43:51,440 --> 00:43:56,940
when I say one hot, by the way, I mean a vector like this

832
00:43:56,940 --> 00:43:58,980
where, if you're going to represent the number 2

833
00:43:58,980 --> 00:44:02,980
you use a one-hot vector

834
00:44:02,980 --> 00:44:06,670
So yeah, there are definitely better ways than just this

835
00:44:06,670 --> 00:44:10,950
This is actually fairly common in a lot of reinforcement

836
00:44:10,950 --> 00:44:13,190
learning methods

837
00:44:13,190 --> 00:44:15,890
For example, one thing you could do

838
00:44:15,890 --> 00:44:17,350
is, you could try to get a language

839
00:44:17,350 --> 00:44:20,990
description of the person or their preferences

840
00:44:20,990 --> 00:44:24,310
You could also-- this gets into stuff

841
00:44:24,310 --> 00:44:26,250
that we'll talk about next week

842
00:44:26,250 --> 00:44:29,190
But you could also use some examples of things

843
00:44:29,190 --> 00:44:31,910
that they like and don't like as part of the task descriptor

844
00:44:31,910 --> 00:44:32,750
as well

845
00:44:32,750 --> 00:44:36,310
And so you get something more like in-context learning

846
00:44:36,310 --> 00:44:40,270
in that case, where zi actually might contain some data

847
00:44:40,270 --> 00:44:42,070
about the task

848
00:44:42,070 --> 00:44:46,510
So yeah, there are some better examples like that

849
00:44:46,510 --> 00:44:48,970
So in terms of this problem setup

850
00:44:48,970 --> 00:44:50,790
let's actually look at an example

851
00:44:50,790 --> 00:44:53,230
So we can just start with multitask imitation learning

852
00:44:53,230 --> 00:44:56,510
And in the multitask imitation learning

853
00:44:56,510 --> 00:45:03,500
setting, we can think of it as, if we have different tasks, i

854
00:45:03,500 --> 00:45:08,100
if the number of tasks, i, is a relatively small number, then

855
00:45:08,100 --> 00:45:11,020
you can basically think of it as having

856
00:45:11,020 --> 00:45:15,540
some set of demonstrations that are demonstration trajectories

857
00:45:15,540 --> 00:45:18,700
for each task, where each of these

858
00:45:18,700 --> 00:45:24,340
contains expert demonstrations

859
00:45:24,340 --> 00:45:30,340
So these are demos for task i

860
00:45:30,340 --> 00:45:42,460
And this is for i equals 1 to N if you have N different tasks

861
00:45:42,460 --> 00:45:45,300
And if we were to think about how

862
00:45:45,300 --> 00:45:49,700
to use this data for imitation learning

863
00:45:49,700 --> 00:45:53,430
thinking back to the second lecture

864
00:45:53,430 --> 00:45:54,800
we were doing imitation learning

865
00:45:54,800 --> 00:45:58,940
We had this objective of minimizing the log probability

866
00:45:58,940 --> 00:46:01,170
of actions' given states

867
00:46:01,170 --> 00:46:07,130
And if we just write this as minimizing the supervised loss

868
00:46:07,130 --> 00:46:11,650
function with respect to our policy parameters

869
00:46:11,650 --> 00:46:15,490
then when we want to move to the multitask setting, what

870
00:46:15,490 --> 00:46:20,610
we can do is, we can simply try to now minimize a single policy

871
00:46:20,610 --> 00:46:24,890
set of policy parameters over the loss function averaged

872
00:46:24,890 --> 00:46:31,810
over all of the tasks

873
00:46:31,810 --> 00:46:35,270
So this is basically like multitask imitation learning

874
00:46:35,270 --> 00:46:39,170
pretty simple, hopefully

875
00:46:39,170 --> 00:46:42,690
One useful trick for this sort of approach

876
00:46:42,690 --> 00:46:48,590
is that if tasks are very different, like if one task is

877
00:46:48,590 --> 00:46:50,170
telling you to go forward, one task is

878
00:46:50,170 --> 00:46:52,010
telling you to go backwards--

879
00:46:52,010 --> 00:46:53,770
maybe one task is telling you to lie down

880
00:46:53,770 --> 00:46:56,530
They're telling the policy to do very different things

881
00:46:56,530 --> 00:46:59,890
Then if you sampled one mini batch that's mostly

882
00:46:59,890 --> 00:47:03,490
going forward and a different mini batch of mostly laying

883
00:47:03,490 --> 00:47:07,470
down, for example, then from one gradient step to the next

884
00:47:07,470 --> 00:47:09,470
you're actually going to have a lot of variance

885
00:47:09,470 --> 00:47:10,570
Because the first mini batch is going

886
00:47:10,570 --> 00:47:11,990
to tell you to do something that's

887
00:47:11,990 --> 00:47:14,190
pretty different from the second mini batch

888
00:47:14,190 --> 00:47:15,650
And hopefully, the policy should be

889
00:47:15,650 --> 00:47:19,210
able to use the task identifier to differentiate the two

890
00:47:19,210 --> 00:47:21,490
But in some cases, it can be a little bit

891
00:47:21,490 --> 00:47:23,970
tricky for the network to do that

892
00:47:23,970 --> 00:47:25,610
And so one thing that's quite useful

893
00:47:25,610 --> 00:47:27,570
is that when you sample a mini batch

894
00:47:27,570 --> 00:47:30,170
you can stratify the mini batch and ensure

895
00:47:30,170 --> 00:47:35,410
that each mini batch of data has data from each task

896
00:47:35,410 --> 00:47:38,450
And if you're trying to optimize for uniform distribution

897
00:47:38,450 --> 00:47:41,450
over your tasks, then your mini batch would be like--

898
00:47:41,450 --> 00:47:43,870
1/n of the data would be the first task

899
00:47:43,870 --> 00:47:47,410
1/n of the mini batch would be the second task and so forth

900
00:47:47,410 --> 00:47:51,090
And this will give you gradients that are lower variance

901
00:47:51,090 --> 00:47:53,810
And it can be quite useful in practice

902
00:47:53,810 --> 00:47:55,660
to do something like this

903
00:47:55,660 --> 00:47:58,120
Another thing that I didn't put on the slides that can also

904
00:47:58,120 --> 00:48:02,120
be useful is making sure that your states and actions are

905
00:48:02,120 --> 00:48:04,220
on similar scales across tasks

906
00:48:04,220 --> 00:48:07,240
So per-task normalization can also

907
00:48:07,240 --> 00:48:13,920
be quite useful for the learning process

908
00:48:13,920 --> 00:48:19,120
Now, we've talked about training this multitask policy

909
00:48:19,120 --> 00:48:22,560
And when we do that, we're now going

910
00:48:22,560 --> 00:48:28,200
to have a policy that takes as input some state and some task

911
00:48:28,200 --> 00:48:34,480
descriptor and then needs to output some action

912
00:48:34,480 --> 00:48:36,400
And you might have scenarios where

913
00:48:36,400 --> 00:48:40,820
this is like a language string or a one-hot vector

914
00:48:40,820 --> 00:48:41,920
and this is an image

915
00:48:41,920 --> 00:48:45,220
And that's inherently a very multimodal problem

916
00:48:45,220 --> 00:48:46,940
You have different data modalities

917
00:48:46,940 --> 00:48:49,010
These are very different dimensions

918
00:48:49,010 --> 00:48:50,760
And so in practice, there's a question of

919
00:48:50,760 --> 00:48:52,760
how do you design architectures that

920
00:48:52,760 --> 00:48:58,390
take as input this kind of multimodal data

921
00:48:58,390 --> 00:49:02,150
One example that I'll give that I like because it actually

922
00:49:02,150 --> 00:49:06,030
uses multiple different modalities of task descriptors

923
00:49:06,030 --> 00:49:09,830
is this paper here, where the goal is a multitask

924
00:49:09,830 --> 00:49:12,310
imitation learning system, is training a robot

925
00:49:12,310 --> 00:49:15,430
to do some simple manipulation tasks

926
00:49:15,430 --> 00:49:18,190
And the way the architecture works--

927
00:49:18,190 --> 00:49:21,430
this paper is a little bit old, so it's not using a transformer

928
00:49:21,430 --> 00:49:23,670
But here's the current state right here

929
00:49:23,670 --> 00:49:25,910
It actually downsamples the image

930
00:49:25,910 --> 00:49:28,430
before passing it into the model

931
00:49:28,430 --> 00:49:31,730
And then it also uses, actually, two different task descriptors

932
00:49:31,730 --> 00:49:35,390
So it uses a language string as one task descriptor, which is

933
00:49:35,390 --> 00:49:37,990
put bottle in the ceramic bowl

934
00:49:37,990 --> 00:49:39,990
And it also trains the model to be

935
00:49:39,990 --> 00:49:42,050
able to, instead of using the language

936
00:49:42,050 --> 00:49:44,890
be able to use a video of how to do the task

937
00:49:44,890 --> 00:49:46,310
In this case, the video is a video

938
00:49:46,310 --> 00:49:51,310
of a person placing the bottle in the bowl

939
00:49:51,310 --> 00:49:56,140
And so you can design models that in this case then

940
00:49:56,140 --> 00:50:01,260
for the language, use a language encoder, like things that

941
00:50:01,260 --> 00:50:04,960
are typically used in language models, for example

942
00:50:04,960 --> 00:50:05,500
transformers

943
00:50:05,500 --> 00:50:07,460
And actually, I think the language encoder here

944
00:50:07,460 --> 00:50:08,980
did actually use a transformer

945
00:50:08,980 --> 00:50:10,760
And then, separately, for the video

946
00:50:10,760 --> 00:50:12,780
it would be using a different kind of encoder

947
00:50:12,780 --> 00:50:14,960
because video data looks very different

948
00:50:14,960 --> 00:50:18,000
And it uses a video encoder for the second part

949
00:50:18,000 --> 00:50:23,580
And then it kind of multiplies these embeddings

950
00:50:23,580 --> 00:50:26,680
into different layers of the network

951
00:50:26,680 --> 00:50:33,700
the backbone of the network that processes the state

952
00:50:33,700 --> 00:50:36,460
And then the loss function corresponds

953
00:50:36,460 --> 00:50:39,020
to a behavior-cloning loss

954
00:50:39,020 --> 00:50:42,540
And it either samples the task descriptor

955
00:50:42,540 --> 00:50:45,420
from the video encoder or from the language encoder

956
00:50:45,420 --> 00:50:50,060
And then the whole model, including these encoders

957
00:50:50,060 --> 00:50:56,620
is trained using the behavior-cloning loss

958
00:50:56,620 --> 00:50:59,900
So this is one practical example

959
00:50:59,900 --> 00:51:02,600
Now, we can also look at some more modern architectures

960
00:51:02,600 --> 00:51:05,700
And first off, if you have something

961
00:51:05,700 --> 00:51:08,800
like a language model, the task descriptor

962
00:51:08,800 --> 00:51:11,380
it's very natural for it just to be a language string

963
00:51:11,380 --> 00:51:13,800
And you can think of that basically just as the prompt

964
00:51:13,800 --> 00:51:18,580
And so one way to think about multitask imitation learning is

965
00:51:18,580 --> 00:51:21,140
basically, all of the language model imitation learning

966
00:51:21,140 --> 00:51:23,520
or supervised fine-tuning is with different prompts

967
00:51:23,520 --> 00:51:26,220
You can sort of think of those as different tasks

968
00:51:26,220 --> 00:51:30,380
And it's very easy to just pass that into a transformer

969
00:51:30,380 --> 00:51:33,380
And in terms of modern policy architectures

970
00:51:33,380 --> 00:51:36,420
for using task strings in robotics

971
00:51:36,420 --> 00:51:39,540
it's often common to also use a transformer

972
00:51:39,540 --> 00:51:43,520
and find ways to use image encoders

973
00:51:43,520 --> 00:51:47,620
in this case, pre-trained image encoders, project

974
00:51:47,620 --> 00:51:51,140
those into the input space of a language model

975
00:51:51,140 --> 00:51:53,810
also use the task descriptor here

976
00:51:53,810 --> 00:51:55,690
which is, what should the robot do to put

977
00:51:55,690 --> 00:51:57,130
the eggplant in the bowl

978
00:51:57,130 --> 00:52:00,170
Pass those in as tokens to a language model

979
00:52:00,170 --> 00:52:03,730
And then output the actions and train the whole model

980
00:52:03,730 --> 00:52:09,330
including the image encoder and the text encoder end to end

981
00:52:09,330 --> 00:52:14,050
So here, you can view the task identifier zi

982
00:52:14,050 --> 00:52:18,210
as basically the prompt for the model

983
00:52:18,210 --> 00:52:19,810
The question is, how does this compare

984
00:52:19,810 --> 00:52:26,110
to traditional reinforcement learning

985
00:52:26,110 --> 00:52:31,850
in terms of task performance and success rate

986
00:52:31,850 --> 00:52:35,150
So I guess there's a couple of things that are different here

987
00:52:35,150 --> 00:52:41,130
We'll talk about multitask RL in a bit, right after this

988
00:52:41,130 --> 00:52:47,040
And then so you can do this sort of prompt conditioning, task

989
00:52:47,040 --> 00:52:49,210
identifier conditioning both with imitation learning

990
00:52:49,210 --> 00:52:51,760
and with reinforcement learning

991
00:52:51,760 --> 00:52:54,240
And generally, the considerations

992
00:52:54,240 --> 00:52:55,760
about imitation learning versus RL

993
00:52:55,760 --> 00:52:58,200
is very similar to what we've discussed so far, where

994
00:52:58,200 --> 00:53:00,320
with imitation learning, you're not

995
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:03,340
able to use a reward function to improve over the expert data

996
00:53:03,340 --> 00:53:05,200
But it's very simple and it's very easy

997
00:53:05,200 --> 00:53:08,160
to scale simple things to large data sets

998
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:11,980
whereas RL has a lot of potential and can help a lot

999
00:53:11,980 --> 00:53:14,240
when you have some reward information

1000
00:53:14,240 --> 00:53:16,420
and you want to optimize those rewards

1001
00:53:16,420 --> 00:53:17,920
And you can actually do a lot better

1002
00:53:17,920 --> 00:53:22,800
than some provided data set

1003
00:53:22,800 --> 00:53:25,360
And then just some examples of what

1004
00:53:25,360 --> 00:53:29,040
multitask imitation learning policies might look like--

1005
00:53:29,040 --> 00:53:33,070
the examples from those two prior architectures--

1006
00:53:33,070 --> 00:53:34,740
the robots are doing very simple things

1007
00:53:34,740 --> 00:53:35,760
And so I wanted to pick something

1008
00:53:35,760 --> 00:53:37,120
that's a bit more recent

1009
00:53:37,120 --> 00:53:41,080
So here, the task identifier is in the top left

1010
00:53:41,080 --> 00:53:42,860
In this video, there'll just be two tasks

1011
00:53:42,860 --> 00:53:45,720
One is to flatten the towel, and one is to hang the towel

1012
00:53:45,720 --> 00:53:49,280
And so for the first part, the task is to flatten the towel

1013
00:53:49,280 --> 00:53:51,280
And then here, we're actually stringing together

1014
00:53:51,280 --> 00:53:52,800
multiple tasks

1015
00:53:52,800 --> 00:53:56,860
So at the point where the robot is at a point where the towel is

1016
00:53:56,860 --> 00:53:59,480
in a more flattened state, the human actually switches the task

1017
00:53:59,480 --> 00:54:03,480
and then tells the robot, now I want you to hang the towel

1018
00:54:03,480 --> 00:54:07,760
And this, again, is passed into a transformer for the robot

1019
00:54:07,760 --> 00:54:09,960
to do that task

1020
00:54:09,960 --> 00:54:12,050
So this is a case where there's two tasks

1021
00:54:12,050 --> 00:54:14,420
You can also do, actually, a lot more tasks, potentially

1022
00:54:14,420 --> 00:54:17,400
So here's an example where there's I think more than 10

1023
00:54:17,400 --> 00:54:19,320
tasks, 10 different language strings

1024
00:54:19,320 --> 00:54:23,520
that the robot follows, so like, put the plate in the drawer

1025
00:54:23,520 --> 00:54:24,500
Close the microwave

1026
00:54:24,500 --> 00:54:25,500
Drive the base backward

1027
00:54:25,500 --> 00:54:26,760
Rotate the base left

1028
00:54:26,760 --> 00:54:28,970
And one of the things that's interesting to note here

1029
00:54:28,970 --> 00:54:31,560
is that tasks can also have different levels of abstraction

1030
00:54:31,560 --> 00:54:33,230
So you might have a level of abstraction

1031
00:54:33,230 --> 00:54:36,500
of just like, drive forward or move your right gripper lower

1032
00:54:36,500 --> 00:54:37,920
Or you might have something that's

1033
00:54:37,920 --> 00:54:41,160
much higher, like throw the napkin away

1034
00:54:41,160 --> 00:54:44,000
or something like that

1035
00:54:44,000 --> 00:54:47,240
The question is, if you had one policy for each task

1036
00:54:47,240 --> 00:54:50,050
would that run similar to this or different

1037
00:54:50,050 --> 00:54:50,550
Yeah

1038
00:54:50,550 --> 00:54:54,050
So in this case, it's one policy with the prompt as input

1039
00:54:54,050 --> 00:54:57,830
You could also have a different policy, one for each task

1040
00:54:57,830 --> 00:54:59,830
If you don't do any--

1041
00:54:59,830 --> 00:55:02,370
what I would call weight sharing, like if you have

1042
00:55:02,370 --> 00:55:03,830
basically, completely different set

1043
00:55:03,830 --> 00:55:05,990
of parameters for every single task

1044
00:55:05,990 --> 00:55:09,230
then you can't really make use of--

1045
00:55:09,230 --> 00:55:11,610
the weights are only trained on data from that one task

1046
00:55:11,610 --> 00:55:13,440
And you're going to have a smaller data set

1047
00:55:13,440 --> 00:55:15,310
And so usually, in many scenarios

1048
00:55:15,310 --> 00:55:18,590
that performs worse than training a single policy

1049
00:55:18,590 --> 00:55:22,030
Another consideration is that it ends up using a lot more memory

1050
00:55:22,030 --> 00:55:23,450
to store all those weights

1051
00:55:23,450 --> 00:55:26,570
And you might actually need to reload the model into memory

1052
00:55:26,570 --> 00:55:30,220
And so it can take longer to switch between different tasks

1053
00:55:30,220 --> 00:55:32,390
But sometimes there are also optimization challenges

1054
00:55:32,390 --> 00:55:34,950
with trying to fit multiple tasks into one model

1055
00:55:34,950 --> 00:55:37,030
And so sometimes there are some circumstances

1056
00:55:37,030 --> 00:55:39,350
where training different policies for different tasks

1057
00:55:39,350 --> 00:55:40,610
can be useful

1058
00:55:40,610 --> 00:55:41,110
Yeah

1059
00:55:41,110 --> 00:55:43,570
So the question is, are these systems hierarchical

1060
00:55:43,570 --> 00:55:47,070
Like, some of these tasks are fairly high level

1061
00:55:47,070 --> 00:55:49,620
So the next part of this slide is

1062
00:55:49,620 --> 00:55:51,350
what about even more high-level tasks

1063
00:55:51,350 --> 00:55:57,660
Like, what if you wanted to tell the robot, clean the kitchen

1064
00:55:57,660 --> 00:55:59,680
For the videos that I've shown here

1065
00:55:59,680 --> 00:56:02,940
this is just one single policy that's doing it

1066
00:56:02,940 --> 00:56:08,040
But hierarchy is very useful for higher-level tasks

1067
00:56:08,040 --> 00:56:10,340
And if you just told this robot to clean the kitchen

1068
00:56:10,340 --> 00:56:14,100
it wouldn't be able to do what it did in this video

1069
00:56:14,100 --> 00:56:16,780
And so we'll have a lecture on hierarchy in, I think

1070
00:56:16,780 --> 00:56:18,540
two-ish weeks

1071
00:56:18,540 --> 00:56:20,900
And we'll talk about exactly that and scenarios

1072
00:56:20,900 --> 00:56:24,900
where we could try to do even higher-level tasks

1073
00:56:24,900 --> 00:56:27,420
But yeah, even with a single policy, a single flat policy

1074
00:56:27,420 --> 00:56:32,580
without hierarchy, it is able to do reasonable actions for tasks

1075
00:56:32,580 --> 00:56:34,780
that are somewhat high level

1076
00:56:34,780 --> 00:56:36,500
What does long-horizon task mean

1077
00:56:36,500 --> 00:56:40,500
By that, I mean a task that takes a long time, basically

1078
00:56:40,500 --> 00:56:42,230
where you takes many time steps

1079
00:56:42,230 --> 00:56:44,020
You have to take a lot of different actions

1080
00:56:44,020 --> 00:56:45,660
in order to do that

1081
00:56:45,660 --> 00:56:47,770
And there's generally a number of challenges

1082
00:56:47,770 --> 00:56:50,210
that can come up with long-horizon tasks, such

1083
00:56:50,210 --> 00:56:56,370
as you have to be thinking about what you should be doing

1084
00:56:56,370 --> 00:56:57,740
in order to complete that

1085
00:56:57,740 --> 00:57:00,110
And there's lots of things you could do to complete that

1086
00:57:00,110 --> 00:57:01,650
So there might be multimodality

1087
00:57:01,650 --> 00:57:03,110
In reinforcement learning settings

1088
00:57:03,110 --> 00:57:05,150
you might also have sparse rewards

1089
00:57:05,150 --> 00:57:07,910
And you might not get reward feedback for a very long time

1090
00:57:07,910 --> 00:57:08,410
Yeah

1091
00:57:08,410 --> 00:57:10,790
So the question is, a lot of these tasks are pretty similar

1092
00:57:10,790 --> 00:57:12,630
What if you wanted a task that's more vision based--

1093
00:57:12,630 --> 00:57:15,850
I think you said

1094
00:57:15,850 --> 00:57:16,350
Yeah

1095
00:57:16,350 --> 00:57:18,730
So this particular model was actually

1096
00:57:18,730 --> 00:57:20,770
also trained to do certain vision tasks

1097
00:57:20,770 --> 00:57:23,450
like asking questions about the image

1098
00:57:23,450 --> 00:57:27,650
and captioning an image, that sort of stuff

1099
00:57:27,650 --> 00:57:31,070
And if you have a big enough model

1100
00:57:31,070 --> 00:57:32,330
it's not too hard to do that

1101
00:57:32,330 --> 00:57:34,170
But generally, you do need larger models

1102
00:57:34,170 --> 00:57:38,490
to be able to do more things

1103
00:57:38,490 --> 00:57:39,370
Cool

1104
00:57:39,370 --> 00:57:41,790
So we've talked a bit about multi-task imitation learning

1105
00:57:41,790 --> 00:57:44,730
Now what about multitask RL

1106
00:57:44,730 --> 00:57:47,890
Basically, the story is pretty similar

1107
00:57:47,890 --> 00:57:51,330
We can feed as input a task descriptor into the policy

1108
00:57:51,330 --> 00:57:54,650
feed as input a task descriptor into a Q function or a value

1109
00:57:54,650 --> 00:57:55,150
function

1110
00:57:55,150 --> 00:57:56,810
Whatever we're learning, we can pass

1111
00:57:56,810 --> 00:58:01,610
in that prompt or some other descriptor of the task

1112
00:58:01,610 --> 00:58:05,330
into the models

1113
00:58:05,330 --> 00:58:08,890
If you have a discrete number of tasks

1114
00:58:08,890 --> 00:58:10,890
then it can be useful to have a different replay

1115
00:58:10,890 --> 00:58:12,850
buffer for each task, especially if you

1116
00:58:12,850 --> 00:58:19,090
want to try to balance the sampling of each batch

1117
00:58:19,090 --> 00:58:23,410
And now there's this question of sharing data

1118
00:58:23,410 --> 00:58:26,610
And this comes up specifically in reinforcement learning

1119
00:58:26,610 --> 00:58:27,830
not in imitation learning

1120
00:58:27,830 --> 00:58:30,330
So in particular, if we collected

1121
00:58:30,330 --> 00:58:35,570
some sort of exploratory data for a task when-- like

1122
00:58:35,570 --> 00:58:38,610
maybe our policy was told to pick up the sponge

1123
00:58:38,610 --> 00:58:40,170
or something like that

1124
00:58:40,170 --> 00:58:41,510
Our policy did something

1125
00:58:41,510 --> 00:58:43,090
It might not have picked up the sponge

1126
00:58:43,090 --> 00:58:45,440
because it might have made a mistake

1127
00:58:45,440 --> 00:58:49,880
That sort of experience might be useful for other task

1128
00:58:49,880 --> 00:58:51,240
descriptors as well

1129
00:58:51,240 --> 00:58:55,340
And if we have a per-task replay buffer, like one for each task

1130
00:58:55,340 --> 00:58:56,800
then we're not actually going to be

1131
00:58:56,800 --> 00:58:59,810
updating the policy for other tasks using that data

1132
00:58:59,810 --> 00:59:01,600
And we could actually think about scenarios

1133
00:59:01,600 --> 00:59:05,720
where we might actually be able to reuse data from one task

1134
00:59:05,720 --> 00:59:12,610
to actually more explicitly help the model or the policy

1135
00:59:12,610 --> 00:59:16,580
learn something about another task

1136
00:59:16,580 --> 00:59:18,160
And if we were to do this, we actually

1137
00:59:18,160 --> 00:59:20,080
have to relabel the data

1138
00:59:20,080 --> 00:59:25,600
So specifically, if the tasks have different reward functions

1139
00:59:25,600 --> 00:59:28,000
but maybe the same dynamics, then you'd

1140
00:59:28,000 --> 00:59:29,480
have to actually change the reward

1141
00:59:29,480 --> 00:59:32,600
label in the data set for it to be the reward

1142
00:59:32,600 --> 00:59:35,000
label for the other task

1143
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:36,660
So let's walk through an example

1144
00:59:36,660 --> 00:59:41,910
So say that you are trying to learn to play hockey

1145
00:59:41,910 --> 00:59:48,030
And there's maybe some teammates in blue

1146
00:59:48,030 --> 00:59:52,110
There is a goalkeeper from the other team in orange

1147
00:59:52,110 --> 00:59:54,930
And maybe one task is passing

1148
00:59:54,930 --> 00:59:57,990
Another task is shooting a goal

1149
00:59:57,990 --> 01:00:02,150
And maybe you are trying to shoot a goal, but you messed up

1150
01:00:02,150 --> 01:00:07,030
and you accidentally actually making a pretty good pass

1151
01:00:07,030 --> 01:00:10,710
to your teammate

1152
01:00:10,710 --> 01:00:13,770
Then that data that you just collected, even though you

1153
01:00:13,770 --> 01:00:16,350
were intending to shoot a goal, that data actually is probably

1154
01:00:16,350 --> 01:00:21,050
pretty useful for learning how to be better at passing

1155
01:00:21,050 --> 01:00:22,630
And so what you could do is, you could

1156
01:00:22,630 --> 01:00:25,470
store that experience as normal under the original task

1157
01:00:25,470 --> 01:00:26,390
identifier

1158
01:00:26,390 --> 01:00:30,990
But you could also relabel the experience with task

1159
01:00:30,990 --> 01:00:35,110
with the task 1 ID, specifically

1160
01:00:35,110 --> 01:00:36,730
the passing ID-- there's a typo there--

1161
01:00:36,730 --> 01:00:41,100
and then store that in the replay buffer for that task

1162
01:00:41,100 --> 01:00:51,060
And so specifically what this algorithm would look like is--

1163
01:00:51,060 --> 01:00:56,620
maybe you pick one of your tasks

1164
01:00:56,620 --> 01:01:00,620
And then we pick a corresponding task identifier

1165
01:01:00,620 --> 01:01:07,340
Then we're going to collect data

1166
01:01:07,340 --> 01:01:12,820
And we'll be collecting data from our policy

1167
01:01:12,820 --> 01:01:14,460
That's going to be then conditioned

1168
01:01:14,460 --> 01:01:20,620
on that task identifier

1169
01:01:20,620 --> 01:01:24,020
And maybe let's refer to this data as--

1170
01:01:24,020 --> 01:01:31,700
it'll have st, at, st plus 1, rt

1171
01:01:31,700 --> 01:01:35,920
And then it will also have the task identifier

1172
01:01:35,920 --> 01:01:41,680
We'll maybe put this into a replay buffer for task i

1173
01:01:41,680 --> 01:01:48,020
So we'll store this in the replay buffer

1174
01:01:48,020 --> 01:01:50,540
And then we could actually then potentially use

1175
01:01:50,540 --> 01:01:53,620
this for another task as well

1176
01:01:53,620 --> 01:01:58,740
So that's what I'm calling relabeling

1177
01:01:58,740 --> 01:02:04,800
And we could consider this data-- so st, at, st plus 1

1178
01:02:04,800 --> 01:02:07,340
But we could actually consider it for a different task, maybe

1179
01:02:07,340 --> 01:02:08,300
task j

1180
01:02:08,300 --> 01:02:11,100
And so the reward for task j would

1181
01:02:11,100 --> 01:02:19,900
be r of j for st, at and the task identifier for task j

1182
01:02:19,900 --> 01:02:22,620
And we could take this data that's been relabeled

1183
01:02:22,620 --> 01:02:26,300
and add this to either our shared replay

1184
01:02:26,300 --> 01:02:30,540
buffer or our replay buffer for task j

1185
01:02:30,540 --> 01:02:34,500
And so then we're actually using experience for one task

1186
01:02:34,500 --> 01:02:39,130
to learn another task

1187
01:02:39,130 --> 01:02:44,970
And then we might do this for multiple tasks

1188
01:02:44,970 --> 01:02:50,330
Eventually, we will update our policy, pi theta

1189
01:02:50,330 --> 01:02:54,110
and collect some more data

1190
01:02:54,110 --> 01:02:54,610
Yeah

1191
01:02:54,610 --> 01:02:57,230
So the question is, how do we decide if it's worth relabeling

1192
01:02:57,230 --> 01:03:00,770
And in particular, if we're doing this for task j

1193
01:03:00,770 --> 01:03:03,200
which task j should we pick

1194
01:03:03,200 --> 01:03:04,370
Should we do it for all j's

1195
01:03:04,370 --> 01:03:06,710
Should we only do it for some j's

1196
01:03:06,710 --> 01:03:09,250
Should we only do this some of the time

1197
01:03:09,250 --> 01:03:12,230
This ends up being somewhat kind of a heuristic process

1198
01:03:12,230 --> 01:03:15,570
Because it's a little bit hard to tell, generally

1199
01:03:15,570 --> 01:03:18,210
if data will be useful for learning one task

1200
01:03:18,210 --> 01:03:20,290
or not because learning is somewhat

1201
01:03:20,290 --> 01:03:23,170
of an amorphous process

1202
01:03:23,170 --> 01:03:28,330
The one heuristic is that if our j was high

1203
01:03:28,330 --> 01:03:30,690
then it might be good for learning that task

1204
01:03:30,690 --> 01:03:34,210
Because you actually might have accidentally done something good

1205
01:03:34,210 --> 01:03:35,690
for a different task

1206
01:03:35,690 --> 01:03:37,760
So that's one heuristic

1207
01:03:37,760 --> 01:03:41,360
Another heuristic sometimes is just to also sample randomly

1208
01:03:41,360 --> 01:03:45,240
and use it for all tasks at random and uniformly

1209
01:03:45,240 --> 01:03:47,880
How does this work for continuous state

1210
01:03:47,880 --> 01:03:51,520
There's nothing here that assumes discrete states

1211
01:03:51,520 --> 01:03:52,660
How do you reach--

1212
01:03:52,660 --> 01:03:57,400
so what I've written here is like a relabeling algorithm

1213
01:03:57,400 --> 01:04:01,280
for any sort of multitask system

1214
01:04:01,280 --> 01:04:04,280
We'll talk about what this looks like for goal-condition settings

1215
01:04:04,280 --> 01:04:07,540
in a minute if that's what you're wondering about

1216
01:04:07,540 --> 01:04:08,040
Yeah

1217
01:04:08,040 --> 01:04:12,240
So this is a great point

1218
01:04:12,240 --> 01:04:14,520
You can't do this universally

1219
01:04:14,520 --> 01:04:18,520
And so this is something that's called hindsight

1220
01:04:18,520 --> 01:04:20,760
relabeling because you look at what you did

1221
01:04:20,760 --> 01:04:22,640
If you did something good, then in hindsight

1222
01:04:22,640 --> 01:04:26,440
you can then relabel that to be a different task

1223
01:04:26,440 --> 01:04:30,080
So in terms of this algorithm on the slides

1224
01:04:30,080 --> 01:04:32,140
we collect some data using some policy

1225
01:04:32,140 --> 01:04:34,760
We store that data in a replay buffer

1226
01:04:34,760 --> 01:04:37,630
We are going to perform hindsight relabeling

1227
01:04:37,630 --> 01:04:40,350
And what this looks like is, we originally

1228
01:04:40,350 --> 01:04:42,190
collected it for task i

1229
01:04:42,190 --> 01:04:47,480
We're going to relabel it with the reward for task j

1230
01:04:47,480 --> 01:04:49,150
And then we'll store that relabeled data

1231
01:04:49,150 --> 01:04:50,510
in a replay buffer

1232
01:04:50,510 --> 01:04:54,860
The slides have this for a single combined replay buffer

1233
01:04:54,860 --> 01:04:57,030
You could also do this with different replay buffers

1234
01:04:57,030 --> 01:04:58,870
for each task

1235
01:04:58,870 --> 01:05:01,270
Then you'll update your policy with the replay buffer

1236
01:05:01,270 --> 01:05:04,670
and repeat this process

1237
01:05:04,670 --> 01:05:06,310
In terms of which tasks to choose

1238
01:05:06,310 --> 01:05:10,410
you can choose this randomly, task to get high rewards

1239
01:05:10,410 --> 01:05:12,030
The note here is also, you can actually

1240
01:05:12,030 --> 01:05:14,090
do it for trajectories that get high rewards

1241
01:05:14,090 --> 01:05:16,350
not just transitions that get high rewards

1242
01:05:16,350 --> 01:05:19,130
Because if there's a high reward somewhere in the trajectory

1243
01:05:19,130 --> 01:05:20,550
then other parts of the trajectory

1244
01:05:20,550 --> 01:05:24,010
might have done something good as well

1245
01:05:24,010 --> 01:05:24,510
Yeah

1246
01:05:24,510 --> 01:05:28,750
And then there's a question of, when can we apply relabeling

1247
01:05:28,750 --> 01:05:31,150
And in particular, there's actually

1248
01:05:31,150 --> 01:05:38,110
restrictions on both the set of MDPs or the MDPs

1249
01:05:38,110 --> 01:05:42,150
of the different tasks and also on the algorithm

1250
01:05:42,150 --> 01:05:45,830
that you're using for reinforcement learning

1251
01:05:45,830 --> 01:05:49,950
So does anyone have thoughts on what

1252
01:05:49,950 --> 01:05:54,750
has to be shared or not shared for this algorithm

1253
01:05:54,750 --> 01:05:56,670
to make sense

1254
01:05:56,670 --> 01:05:58,510
Yeah, so a shared state action space

1255
01:05:58,510 --> 01:06:09,150
So you're going to want a shared state and action space

1256
01:06:09,150 --> 01:06:14,130
Anything else do you want that's shared and not shared

1257
01:06:14,130 --> 01:06:14,630
Yeah

1258
01:06:14,630 --> 01:06:15,910
So if you only have one replay buffer

1259
01:06:15,910 --> 01:06:18,160
then you want the dimensions of your state and actions

1260
01:06:18,160 --> 01:06:22,710
to match and, generally, for the state space and action

1261
01:06:22,710 --> 01:06:24,290
space to be the same

1262
01:06:24,290 --> 01:06:24,790
Yeah

1263
01:06:24,790 --> 01:06:27,350
So you probably want the initial state distribution

1264
01:06:27,350 --> 01:06:28,750
to be similar

1265
01:06:28,750 --> 01:06:43,340
So you want p of s1 to be similar for different tasks

1266
01:06:43,340 --> 01:06:46,780
Because if it's drastically different, then probably

1267
01:06:46,780 --> 01:06:49,460
sharing data won't be as useful

1268
01:06:49,460 --> 01:06:52,140
So if one of them has dense rewards and one of them

1269
01:06:52,140 --> 01:06:56,340
has sparse rewards, that might be a problem

1270
01:06:56,340 --> 01:06:59,220
That is true

1271
01:06:59,220 --> 01:07:02,320
Although, that might be a problem generally in multitask

1272
01:07:02,320 --> 01:07:05,920
RL

1273
01:07:05,920 --> 01:07:07,380
But actually, in this case, I don't

1274
01:07:07,380 --> 01:07:08,380
think it's going to be especially

1275
01:07:08,380 --> 01:07:09,020
an issue in this case

1276
01:07:09,020 --> 01:07:11,020
Because you are going to be using the reward

1277
01:07:11,020 --> 01:07:12,120
function for the task

1278
01:07:12,120 --> 01:07:13,920
But yeah, generally, for multitask RL

1279
01:07:13,920 --> 01:07:15,680
that makes things challenging

1280
01:07:15,680 --> 01:07:16,180
Yeah

1281
01:07:16,180 --> 01:07:18,530
So it's helpful for the rewards to be the same scale

1282
01:07:18,530 --> 01:07:22,420
What about the dynamics

1283
01:07:22,420 --> 01:07:25,420
Should they be the same or different across tasks

1284
01:07:25,420 --> 01:07:26,480
Same, yeah

1285
01:07:26,480 --> 01:07:28,560
So you want the dynamics to be the same

1286
01:07:28,560 --> 01:07:30,100
And that's actually really important

1287
01:07:30,100 --> 01:07:33,310
Because you're not relabeling the dynamics here

1288
01:07:33,310 --> 01:07:35,230
And because you're not relabeling the dynamics

1289
01:07:35,230 --> 01:07:40,450
if they're different across tasks, then yeah

1290
01:07:40,450 --> 01:07:41,570
it's going to be a problem

1291
01:07:41,570 --> 01:07:44,450
And so this is only applicable-- because you're

1292
01:07:44,450 --> 01:07:46,970
teaching the model and sharing data with the dynamics

1293
01:07:46,970 --> 01:07:49,610
from the other task, it's really important for the dynamics

1294
01:07:49,610 --> 01:07:51,970
to be the same

1295
01:07:51,970 --> 01:07:57,210
So the dynamics should be the same

1296
01:07:57,210 --> 01:08:00,090
And then this is also most useful if the reward functions

1297
01:08:00,090 --> 01:08:01,170
are different

1298
01:08:01,170 --> 01:08:04,930
Otherwise, the data is already in the buffer

1299
01:08:04,930 --> 01:08:07,810
with the original reward function

1300
01:08:07,810 --> 01:08:13,210
My last question is, do you need this to be an off-policy RL

1301
01:08:13,210 --> 01:08:19,490
algorithm, or is it OK if this is on policy

1302
01:08:19,490 --> 01:08:21,290
Any thoughts on that

1303
01:08:21,290 --> 01:08:22,290
We'd like to have cover

1304
01:08:22,290 --> 01:08:23,910
So off policy is generally good

1305
01:08:23,910 --> 01:08:25,649
Yeah

1306
01:08:25,649 --> 01:08:31,920
So the action here--

1307
01:08:31,920 --> 01:08:35,560
this was the action that was taken by the policy

1308
01:08:35,560 --> 01:08:38,439
when conditioned on zi

1309
01:08:38,439 --> 01:08:40,479
And when conditioned on zj, the policy

1310
01:08:40,479 --> 01:08:42,439
might have done something different

1311
01:08:42,439 --> 01:08:44,439
And so this data that we're relabeling

1312
01:08:44,439 --> 01:08:47,240
is going to be off-policy data

1313
01:08:47,240 --> 01:08:51,200
And so that means that when we're updating pi theta

1314
01:08:51,200 --> 01:08:53,880
we really need to be using an off-policy algorithm

1315
01:08:53,880 --> 01:08:57,240
because this data is not following the policy conditioned

1316
01:08:57,240 --> 01:08:59,359
on zj

1317
01:08:59,359 --> 01:09:00,180
Yeah, exactly

1318
01:09:00,180 --> 01:09:02,359
So if you're going to be using importance weights

1319
01:09:02,359 --> 01:09:04,600
you need to store which policy collected

1320
01:09:04,600 --> 01:09:06,920
the data in the first place

1321
01:09:06,920 --> 01:09:10,380
Well, actually, sorry

1322
01:09:10,380 --> 01:09:16,640
Let me think about that

1323
01:09:16,640 --> 01:09:20,420
This data is actually changed from the original data

1324
01:09:20,420 --> 01:09:24,939
And so actually, for the importance weights

1325
01:09:24,939 --> 01:09:29,960
you'll actually want to use zj, not zi, as the old policy

1326
01:09:29,960 --> 01:09:32,439
Because you're actually kind of creating synthetic data

1327
01:09:32,439 --> 01:09:35,960
that is as if you were using zj

1328
01:09:35,960 --> 01:09:36,460
So yeah

1329
01:09:36,460 --> 01:09:37,800
Sorry, I misspoke

1330
01:09:37,800 --> 01:09:41,319
So you don't need to store which policy it came from

1331
01:09:41,319 --> 01:09:42,580
